## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical testing of dynamic feature flags and edge propagation, race conditions, and rollback paths under real-world latency. It requires concrete test design, tooling, and observability considerations.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant feature flags and canary
- Edge propagation latency and cache invalidation
- Race condition testing during toggles
- Rollback and observability (metrics/logs)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test sketch
test('canary flag propagation across edge', async () => {
  setFlag('TenantA','newContent',true);
  await wait(35000);
  const rA = await httpGet('/content?tenant=TenantA', headers);
  const rB = await httpGet('/content?tenant=TenantB', headers);
  expect(rA.flag).toBe(true);
  expect(rB.flag).toBe(false);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you automate clock manipulation in test env to simulate propagation windows?
- How would you measure edge propagation latency and flag consistency across regions?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests an asynchronous, idempotent flow common in payments; exercises end-to-end behavior, including 202 semantics, Location header, polling, and fault tolerance.

## Key Concepts
- Idempotency keys prevent duplicate side effects
- Asynchronous processing and status polling
- Proper error handling for missing/invalid headers
- Downstream failure resilience with preserved idempotency

## Code Example
```javascript
// Jest-like sketch
test('idempotent POST /payments', async () => {
  const key = 'idem-123';
  const r1 = await post('/payments', { amount: 100 }, { 'Idempotency-Key': key });
  const r2 = await post('/payments', { amount: 100 }, { 'Idempotency-Key': key });
  expect(r1.status).toBe(202);
  expect(r1.headers.Location).toContain('/payments/');
  expect(r2.status).toBe(202);
  expect(r2.body.id).toBe(r1.body.id);
  // poll until final state
  const id = r1.body.id;
  let s = await get(`/payments/${id}/status`);
  while (s.state === 'pending') s = await get(`/payments/${id}/status`);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend tests for long-running payments and timeouts?
- How would you test idempotency across multiple regions or microservices?

## Why This Is Asked
Payments APIs rely on idempotency to avoid duplicates; beginners must reason about retries, concurrency, and race conditions.

## Key Concepts
- Idempotency keys
- Retry semantics and concurrency
- Conflict handling with identical keys
- Downstream gateway behavior and fallbacks

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example test outline (pseudo)
test('idempotent payment', async () => {
  const key = 'abc-123';
  const payload = {amount: 100, currency: 'USD', customerId: 'C1'};
  const r1 = await post('/payments', payload, {'Idempotency-Key': key});
  const r2 = await post('/payments', payload, {'Idempotency-Key': key});
  assert(r1.paymentId === r2.paymentId);
});
```

