## Why This Is Asked

Tests for idempotent POSTs prevent duplicate orders when clients retry. It shows understanding of race conditions, state management, and how to validate that retries are safe across failure modes.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotency keys
- Duplicate detection and database invariants
- Retry semantics and safe side effects
- Observability and tooling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example idempotent POST test (pseudo)
async function testIdempotentPost() {
  const key = 'abcd-1234';
  const payload = { customerId: 'c1', items: [{sku:'A', qty:1}] };
  const r1 = await fetch('/orders', { method:'POST', headers:{'Idempotency-Key': key}, body: JSON.stringify(payload) });
  const r2 = await fetch('/orders', { method:'POST', headers:{'Idempotency-Key': key}, body: JSON.stringify(payload) });
  // asserts: r1.status==201, r2.status==200 and r1.json().orderId===r2.json().orderId
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test idempotency across checkout and payment services sharing the same key?
- How would you handle key expiry and rotation to avoid stale duplicates?

## Why This Is Asked

Streaming APIs combined with REST surfaces are common in large-scale systems. This question probes testing of delivery guarantees, ordering, resilience under partial outages, and observability—areas critical for Snap, Microsoft, and Google-scale services.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end testing across REST and WebSocket channels
- Message ordering and sequencing guarantees
- Delivery semantics (at-least-once vs exactly-once) and replay safety
- Reconnection, backoff, and backfill strategies using durable offsets
- Handling partial upstream outages and dynamic schema changes
- Observability with tracing, logs, and metrics

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: basic WebSocket stream ordering test (conceptual)
const WebSocket = require('ws');
const ws = new WebSocket('wss://example.com/stream');
let lastSeq = -1;
let ok = true;
let count = 0;
ws.on('message', (data) => {
  const msg = JSON.parse(data);
  if (typeof msg.seq !== 'number' || msg.seq <= lastSeq) ok = false;
  lastSeq = msg.seq;
  count++;
  if (count >= 100) ws.close();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you simulate network partitions and validate reconnect/backoff behavior across regions?
- How would you validate exactly-once semantics when an event is replayed during backfill?

## Why This Is Asked
Introduces an angle not covered: rate-limiting, pagination and input validation in a single endpoint, a common real-world pattern not covered by existing questions.

## Key Concepts
- Input validation and schema contracts
- Rate limiting and Retry-After semantics
- Pagination tokens (cursor) semantics and consistency
- Idempotency and concurrency
- Tooling: Postman, Newman, pytest-requests, k6 for load; mocking rate limiter; contract tests with JSON Schema

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example test: invalid input returns 400
const axios = require('axios');
test('POST /search invalid type returns 400', async () => {
  const res = await axios.post('https://api.example.com/search', { filters: 1, sort: 'asc' }, { validateStatus: () => true });
  expect(res.status).toBe(400);
});
```

